1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,123
Understanding BIDS.

2
00:00:01,123 --> 00:00:07,298
Read standardized neuroimaging filenames and find anatomy, function, events, and metadata.

3
00:00:07,298 --> 00:00:12,912
The goal is clear: Identify BIDS entities in a filename.

4
00:00:13,762 --> 00:00:16,660
Structure is part of the data.

5
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:30,664
The Brain Imaging Data Structure (BIDS) uses predictable folders and filenames so people and software can identify participants, sessions, tasks, runs, and modalities without relying on local naming conventions.

6
00:00:30,664 --> 00:00:35,493
A functional run lives under sub-*/func/ and pairs a _bold.

7
00:00:35,493 --> 00:00:35,976
nii.

8
00:00:35,976 --> 00:00:38,390
gz image with a _bold.

9
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:39,356
json sidecar.

10
00:00:39,356 --> 00:00:40,322
Its _events.

11
00:00:40,322 --> 00:00:43,702
tsv file describes event onsets and durations.

12
00:00:43,702 --> 00:00:47,082
Anatomical T1-weighted data typically live under sub-*/anat/.

13
00:00:47,932 --> 00:00:51,433
Now connect the idea to an observable check.

14
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:53,621
First, list one participant folder.

15
00:00:53,621 --> 00:00:57,559
Next, find one bold image and its JSON sidecar.

16
00:00:57,559 --> 00:00:59,747
Then, find the matching events.

17
00:00:59,747 --> 00:01:00,623
tsv file.

18
00:01:00,623 --> 00:01:04,561
After that, translate each filename entity into plain language.

19
00:01:04,561 --> 00:01:08,062
The command shown on screen is an example.

20
00:01:08,062 --> 00:01:14,626
Adapt its path to your own working directory and verify the target before running it.

21
00:01:15,476 --> 00:01:17,788
Quality control comes before interpretation.

22
00:01:17,788 --> 00:01:25,189
Before interpreting the result, confirm that the input, operation, and output support the same scientific question.

23
00:01:25,189 --> 00:01:32,590
A useful self-check is this: Why does a bold run need both NIfTI and JSON files?

24
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:43,229
The NIfTI stores image data; the JSON sidecar stores acquisition metadata that may not be represented reliably in the image filename or header.

25
00:01:44,079 --> 00:01:48,824
To recap: First, BIDS makes inputs discoverable and machine-readable.

26
00:01:48,824 --> 00:01:54,096
Next, match images, sidecars, and events by their shared entities.

27
00:01:54,096 --> 00:02:02,004
Before continuing, make sure you can explain this objective: Identify BIDS entities in a filename.
